# monitoring/.env — copy to `.env` (same directory) before `docker compose up`.
#
#   cp monitoring/.env.example monitoring/.env
#
# Compose reads this file automatically because it sits next to compose.yaml. It
# holds the *deployment* knobs (ports, the upstream this box watches, retention,
# resource ceilings, the Grafana password). Alert thresholds are NOT here: they
# live in prometheus/alert_thresholds.env and are rendered into
# prometheus/rules.yml, because Prometheus does not expand environment variables
# in rule files (see "Which knobs live where" in monitoring/README.md).
#
# `.env` is machine-local and is NOT committed (see .gitignore); this example
# file is the committed documentation of every knob. The password below is a
# placeholder on purpose: compose fails fast with `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD not
# set` rather than starting with a known password.

# --- the upstream model server -----------------------------------------------
# The one line that says what this box is monitoring. It is a *declaration*, and
# three places have to agree with it; a mismatch is the single most likely way to
# end up with a Grafana that is quietly looking at the wrong server:
#
#   1. prometheus/prometheus.yml job `llm-server`  -> the target list in
#      prometheus/targets/llm-servers.json (Prometheus cannot expand ${VAR} in
#      scrape targets, so the target lives in that file, not here -- see "Why the
#      target list is a file, not a .env variable" in monitoring/README.md).
#   2. prometheus/prometheus.yml job `llm-server-via-proxy` -> the
#      `llm_upstream` label, which becomes the proxy's ?url= parameter.
#   3. monitor/serve.py, if it runs: `LLM_MONITOR_UPSTREAM` in
#      monitor/llm-monitor.service, or its --url argument.
#
# Keep them equal. `python3 monitoring/check_monitoring.py` diffs this value against the
# two Prometheus places and fails on drift, and CI (workspace-szw.8) runs it.
LLM_UPSTREAM_URL=http://llm.home:8001

# The monitor's own passthrough, used by the `llm-server-via-proxy` job when the
# model server is not reachable from the container network. Inside the prometheus
# container `host.docker.internal` is the host (compose.yaml extra_hosts); the
# port must be the one serve.py actually binds (default 8090, loopback).
# Documented the same way: the value Prometheus uses is the `targets:` line of
# that job in prometheus.yml, which also cannot take ${VAR}; `check_monitoring.py` fails if
# the two disagree.
MONITOR_PROXY_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8090

# --- ports (all loopback-only by default) ------------------------------------
# Change these only if something on this host must reach the stack by a different
# port. Publishing them on the LAN is a separate, deliberate decision
# (ticket workspace-szw.12), not a value in this file.
PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090
GRAFANA_PORT=3000
# ALERTMANAGER_PORT=9093

# --- Grafana -----------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED (compose refuses to start without it). Anything but the placeholder.
#
# Rotating it: change the value here, then
#   docker compose -f monitoring/compose.yaml up -d grafana
# (compose recreates the container). If Grafana has already written the admin
# user to its database, the *stored* hash wins over this variable, so also reset
# it once inside the box:
#   docker compose -f monitoring/compose.yaml exec grafana \
#     grafana cli admin reset-admin-password 'new password'
# That is the one and only command in this whole stack that touches the UI's
# state; everything else is a file in git.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# Set when Grafana is served through a reverse proxy under a path, e.g.
# https://llm.home/grafana/ (that also needs GF_SERVER_SERVE_FROM_SUB_PATH, which
# belongs with the LAN/proxy exposure ticket, not here).
# GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=http://localhost:3000/

# --- retention (both caps are mandatory on this box) -------------------------
# Time cap: how far back the 7-day questions can be answered (plus a margin).
PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_TIME=15d
# Size cap: the hard ceiling. Prometheus deletes whole blocks once the TSDB
# exceeds this, so the box can never be wedged by its own telemetry. The math
# behind the number is in monitoring/README.md#retention-sizing.
PROMETHEUS_RETENTION_SIZE=2GB

# --- resource ceilings -------------------------------------------------------
# These are the budget in docs/adr/0001 C5, which workspace-szw.9 measures and
# confirms (monitoring/README.md#footprint). memswap_limit is pinned to
# mem_limit in compose.yaml so a burst cannot eat the model's page cache
# instead -- the whole point of the ttm.pages_limit hack in the root README.
PROMETHEUS_MEM_LIMIT=512m
PROMETHEUS_CPUS=0.50
GRAFANA_MEM_LIMIT=256m
GRAFANA_CPUS=0.25
# Process-count ceilings: a runaway restart loop is caught here rather than by
# the OOM killer eating the model's memory.
PROMETHEUS_PIDS_LIMIT=256
GRAFANA_PIDS_LIMIT=512
